gle.</p><p><strong>Greg Dulcich</strong> has room to work \u2014 CIN allow <strong>46 yds/game to TE1s</strong> (vs ~35), a receiving-yards and target angle.</p><h3>Touchdown Board</h3><p><strong>Greg Dulcich</strong> is a live anytime-TD dart \u2014 CIN are leaky to tight ends in the end zone (0.47 TE TDs/game vs ~0.24).</p><p>In the red zone CIN throw it (64% pass inside the 20) \u2014 <strong>Ja'Marr Chase</strong>'s TD equity over a goal-line back.</p><p>In the red zone MIA throw it (55% pass inside the 20) \u2014 <strong>Kevin Coleman Jr.</strong>'s TD equity over a goal-line back.</p><h3>Game Script, Pace & Environment</h3><p>A modest lean to CIN \u2014 they should lead more often than not, nudging them toward the run late and giving <strong>Chase Brown</strong> a gentle game-script tailwind.</p><h3>CIN \u2014 a <em>pass-heavy</em> offense</h3><p>CIN stay aggressive through the air even with a lead \u2014 they pass <strong>49%</strong> on early downs when ahead versus the league's 44%. They don't sit on the ball, which keeps their receivers live in scripts where most offenses would be running out the clock.</p><p><strong>Chase Brown</strong> (17 carries, 74 yards projected) gets a mild game-script tailwind as a favorite, though CIN's willingness to keep throwing when ahead caps how much the lead converts into extra carries.</p><p><strong>Ja'Marr Chase</strong> projects for <strong>12 targets and 105 yards</strong>, in line with his established role in a script that neither inflates nor suppresses the pass.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Mike Gesicki</strong> (TE, 3 targets, 25 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>MIA \u2014 a <em>run-heavy</em> offense</h3><p>MIA are one of the league's more run-committed offenses: on early downs while <strong>leading</strong>, they pass just <strong>40%</strong> of the time, against a league average of 44% \u2014 a genuine clock-bleeding identity, not noise (it holds after shrinking their sample toward the league).</p><p>Game script works against the run here \u2014 MIA project to trail and throw, which squeezes <strong>De'Von Achane</strong>'s rushing projection (15 carries, 83 yards) but <em>raises</em> his value as a receiver; trailing teams check the ball down, and his target share ticks up as the game gets away from them.</p><p><strong>Kevin Coleman Jr.</strong> is the one to target from a volume standpoint: as underdogs throwing to keep pace, MIA funnel work to their top receiver \u2014 <strong>4 targets for 31 yards</strong> projected, with genuine upside if they fall behind early and the game turns one-dimensional.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Greg Dulcich</strong> (TE, 5 targets, 36 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>What to watch</h3><p>The single biggest swing factor is <strong>availability</strong> \u2014 use the injury tool in the sidebar to mark anyone out; a starter's absence doesn't just remove his line, it <em>redistributes</em> his targets and carries to specific teammates, and the report and projections rebuild around it. Beyond that: if this game breaks toward the projected 6-point margin, the <strong>garbage-time backups</strong> (the \u25c6-flagged rows below) are where soft lines and real edge tend to appear \u2014 those depth players get fed in blowouts, and the market is slowest to price them.</p><p><em>This preview and the projections below regenerate through the week as injuries, weather and line moves update the inputs.</em></p></div>","teams":[{"team":"CIN","col":"#FB4F14","players":[{"name":"Joe Burrow","pos":"QB","starter":true,"minor":false,"markets":[{"label":"Pass yards","proj":242.72,"book":null,"over_th":226.64,"under_th":250.76,"over_am":null,"under_am":null,"pover":null,"cal